\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf  {a}) Change in cooperation per decade of the payoff scale $\lambda $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf  {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf  {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph  {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed. Three of four filled markers lie left of zero: the instruction moves behaviour opposite to its semantic content.}}{7}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:invariance}{{2}{7}{\textbf {Three manipulations that cannot matter, and all three do.} (\textbf {a}) Change in cooperation per decade of the payoff scale $\lam $, with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals resampling whole dyads. A positive affine rescaling leaves the game strategically identical, so the null is exactly zero. Claude and GPT-4o depart from it by more than $0.11$ per decade; the annotation gives the total swing across the three scales. (\textbf {b}) Language disparity, the gap between a model's highest- and lowest-cooperation language. Pale bars use all five languages, in which objective framing varies with language; solid bars use only English, French and Vietnamese, which share a single framing. Dashes mark the 95th percentile of a permutation null; $P$ values are for the framing-controlled comparison. (\textbf {c}) Effect of the assigned persona. Filled markers give the difference between an agent told it is cooperative and one told it is selfish; open markers give the same difference for the \emph {opponent's} persona, which is never disclosed. Three of four filled markers lie left of zero: the instruction moves behaviour opposite to its semantic content}{figure.caption.3}{}}
